  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/9/9a/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_8.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Spider-Man shows up at Dorrie Evans house, seemingly for no other reason than to \"show her what she's missing by not dating Spider-Man!\"&nbsp; But lo and behold, he finds a party about to start, with Johnny Storm in attendance, arriving in a hotrod car, no less.\n\nMoments later, after seeing Johnny showing off his flame powers to his friends, Spider-Man essentially crashes the party, challenging the Torch to fight outside.\n\n\nand seems to barge in on the party Johnny Storm is having with his friends.&nbsp; He crashes it and essentially challenges the Torch to a fight outside.&nbsp; The two of them go back and forth in a largely meaningless tussle until Spider-Man lands to find the other three members of the FF casually watching.&nbsp; When Mr. Fantastic stretches an arm out to help Spider-Man up, he interprets it as an attack and strikes back, now engaging all 4 of them!&nbsp; \"I'll clobber you <i>all</i>!\"\n\nBut thankfully Sue's level head wins out, and she makes everyone realize how senseless the fight is.&nbsp; Spider-Man leaves, telling Johnny that it's a good thing he has the other three around \"to wet-nurse you!\"&nbsp; As for Sue, \"<i>she's</i> the only <i>good</i> thing about the overrated Fantastic Four!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=FOR THE RECORD:]\n[BR] This is a review of the <b>second</b> of the two stories found in this magazine.\n[BR] My review on the other story is posted elsewhere.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIn this tale, artist Jack Kirby is on pencils with Steve Ditko inking, which is the same artistic team that brought the readers this past summer's Strange Tales annual ([LINK:1963:ST_Annual_2_torch0|ST Annual 2|2026-11-01]).&nbsp; So while the artwork was adequate enough (and certainly allowed the various member of the Fantastic Four to largely appear as they normally do in their own magazine), the story didn't have the same level of care.\n\nIn a word, this entire attempt at once again pitting Spider-Man against the Human Torch was:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=poor -- just flat out poor.]\n\nIt's not even that the fight between Spider-Man and the Torch was bad, and the concluding brief tussle between Spider-Man and the rest of the FF was technically about what you'd expect -- it was just that\n[HANG] * We just <i>saw</i> a battle between Spider-Man and the Torch only four months ago,\n[HANG] * but more importantly, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=this entire story was staged by a <i>completely unbelievable premise!</i>]\n\n[ASSET: left | SpiderManTaunt | width=19%] It feels completely out of character for Spider-Man to simply show up at the Torch's party just to mess with him.&nbsp; Had it been a <b>random</b> encounter while he was out doing something else, well then.. [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=sure...] I might buy that, as he might have simply wanted to take advantage of the chance encounter to mess with Johnny's mind and keep him humble (as I <i>do</i> think Spider-Man sees Johnny Storm as a glory hog after all), but it's obvious from the first panel that he went [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=out of his way] to hunt down Johnny and try to embarrass him in front of his friends, seemingly out of nothing more than spite.\n\nI'm sorry, but that is not the Peter Parker that we've come to know and care about in these stories.&nbsp; Remember, he grew up being picked on, so it's quite a stretch to imagine that he'd show any desire to pay that forward.&nbsp; Maybe Jack Kirby just wanted another chance to draw him fighting the FF after he was mostly relegated to re-drawing their original encounter in the earlier annual (remember, that was its <i>second</i> story).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=I don't know.]\n\n[ASSET: left | SpiderManVersusTorch | width=12%] [ASSET: right | BetterQuitNow | width=12%] I acknowledge that Kirby is more than capable of pulling off a nice fight scene between Spider-Man and the Torch, and the two have indeed come into contact a few times in the last few months.\n[BR]\n[BR] When you have two teenaged heroes, I guess that sort of thing is to be expected (by readers, if not by the Marvel Bullpen itself).\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis story was doomed from the first panel and seemed to be staged for nothing more than show.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Bad move, leading directly to a lower grade!]\n\nChristine Cassello's letter published in <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> 11 (ASM #11) echoes my view here when she points out that \"the party-crashing was what I'd expect from <i>Torch</i>\" (emphasis mine).\n\nNot only that, but she also questions Peter being with Betty when she asks \"how old is Jameson's secretary?\"&nbsp; Heh, <i>another</i> point I've made!&nbsp; (However, the Announcements section of ASM #12 explains that Betty is actually <i>younger</i> than Peter, having had to leave school and get a job because she needed the money, intending to complete her education when she gets a chance.&nbsp; By the way, that announcement goes on to declare Peter as a \"high school senior.\")\n\nAnd Wayne Warfield's letter, also printed in issue 12, points out that Spider-Man barging in on Torch's party \"made Spider-Man look like a trouble-maker.\"&nbsp; Richard Vincent also agreed, saying that \"You made Spider-Man seem like a conceited show-off.&nbsp; Please don't have him crashing any more parties!\"\n\nI am forced to agree.&nbsp; The encounter showing Spider-Man barging in on a party -- just to be a nuisance -- goes against everything that \"puny\" Parker must have been forced to endure for years before he ever learned the lesson about what must also come along with great power.\n\n[ASSET: left | FFshowUp | width=12%] [ASSET: right | SpiderManExit | width=12%] While the actual fight scenes were technically adequate, the brief skirmish against the rest of the Fantastic Four even nice (and showed how well Spider-Man could hold his own even against an entire team), and the show of respect for the Invisible Girl at the end well deserved, they only played a small role in lessening the [SPAN: severity=error; text=damage already done] and kept the book's final grade from sinking any lower.\n\nBut in the end, it was still a blatant, in-your-face misrepresentation of what being Spider-Man is all about, and the fact he comes across as a bully to the one hero who actually stood up for him only four months earlier in the eyes of the police only causes my eyes to widen even more!\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger;&nbsp; fonttype=bold; text=Let's face it:&nbsp; this was a bad move on the parts of all involved!]",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}